# Netlify takes the FIRST matching rule, and a path-only rule matches on any
# hostname — so the host redirects have to come before them, or a request to
# the Netlify address would be served by the /admin rewrite below and never
# reach the redirect.

# The site answers on two hostnames: the custom domain and the Netlify one it
# was first deployed to. Anyone arriving on the Netlify address stays there,
# because every link in the site is relative — which is how the admin panel
# kept showing up as melodic-dragon-614a92.netlify.app/admin. Sending the old
# host to the real one fixes that for every page at once, and stops search
# engines treating the two as separate copies of the same site.
# Deploy previews live on deploy-preview-<n>--melodic-dragon-614a92.netlify.app,
# a different hostname, so they are not caught by this and keep working.
http://melodic-dragon-614a92.netlify.app/*   https://villagefen.co.il/:splat   301!
https://melodic-dragon-614a92.netlify.app/*  https://villagefen.co.il/:splat   301!

# Clean URLs. A 200 rewrite serves the file without changing the address bar,
# so the celebrations page lives at /celebrations in both languages
# (/celebrations?lang=he and /celebrations?lang=en are the hreflang targets;
# query strings are forwarded automatically).
/celebrations   /celebrations.html   200

# The admin panel lives at /admin. Netlify's pretty-URL handling already
# serves admin.html there; stating it here means the address keeps working
# even if that build setting is ever turned off.
# Deliberately no 301 from /admin.html back to /admin: combined with the
# rewrite above that can loop, and the deploy preview is password-protected
# so it cannot be proven safe before going live. /admin.html stays reachable.
/admin          /admin.html          200
